{"@context":{"lean":"lean:","block":"block:","imports":{"@type":"@id"}},"@graph":[{"@id":"lean:module:QOU.Archimedean.MultiGeneratorSpectrum","@type":"LeanModule","name":"QOU.Archimedean.MultiGeneratorSpectrum","imports":["lean:module:Init","lean:module:QOU.Archimedean.GroundRing","lean:module:QOU.Archimedean.TransferMatrix","lean:module:QOU.Archimedean.YoungIdempotent","lean:module:QOU.Archimedean.MassGapGm","lean:module:Mathlib.Tactic.Ring"],"declarations":17,"chunks":1},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Archimedean.channelRatioDeterminesGA_def","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Archimedean.channelRatioDeterminesGA_def","kind":"instance","line":233,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Archimedean/MultiGeneratorSpectrum.lean#L233-L237","doc":"Default instance for the trivial coincidence\n`gA = stdEig / symEig` (i.e. when `gA` is *defined* as the\nratio rather than asserted to equal it). ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Archimedean.ChannelRatioDeterminesGA.ratio_eq","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Archimedean.ChannelRatioDeterminesGA.ratio_eq","kind":"theorem","line":231,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Archimedean/MultiGeneratorSpectrum.lean#L231-L231","doc":"The eigenvalue ratio agrees with `g_A`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Archimedean.ChannelRatioDeterminesGA.mk","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Archimedean.ChannelRatioDeterminesGA.mk","kind":"ctor","line":229,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Archimedean/MultiGeneratorSpectrum.lean#L229-L229","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Archimedean.ChannelRatioDeterminesGA","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Archimedean.ChannelRatioDeterminesGA","kind":"class","line":224,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Archimedean/MultiGeneratorSpectrum.lean#L224-L231","doc":"**Channel ratio determines `g_A` — typeclass.**\nPer-instance witness that the standard/symmetric channel\neigenvalue ratio of a multi-generator product equals the axial\ncoupling suppression factor. Pending the categorical product\neigenvalue formula. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Archimedean.multi_pp_alt_eigenvalue","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Archimedean.multi_pp_alt_eigenvalue","kind":"theorem","line":191,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Archimedean/MultiGeneratorSpectrum.lean#L191-L199","doc":"For `k` pure pp crossings, the alternating eigenvalue is `(−q⁻¹)^k`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Archimedean.multi_pp_sym_eigenvalue","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Archimedean.multi_pp_sym_eigenvalue","kind":"theorem","line":165,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Archimedean/MultiGeneratorSpectrum.lean#L165-L189","doc":"**Theorem (Multi-generator channel divergence).**\n\nFor a multi-generator product, the ratio between the symmetric\nand alternating eigenvalues is:\n`lamSym / lamAlt = ∏_j ((c_j·q + d_j) / (d_j − c_j·q⁻¹))`\n\nFor `k` pure pp crossings `(1, 0)`:\n`lamSym = q^k`, `lamAlt = (−q⁻¹)^k = (−1)^k · q^{−k}`\n`lamSym / lamAlt = (−1)^k · q^{2k}`\n\nThis ratio **diverges** as `k → ∞` (for `q > 1`), showing that\nthe channels become increasingly separated for longer braid words.\n\nFor the standard channel, the ratio is more complex because it\ninvolves the 2×2 matrix product, which depends on the specific\nbraid topology (not just the length). ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Archimedean.per_generator_eigenvalue_coincidence","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Archimedean.per_generator_eigenvalue_coincidence","kind":"theorem","line":141,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Archimedean/MultiGeneratorSpectrum.lean#L141-L161","doc":"**Theorem (Per-generator eigenvalue coincidence).**\n\nFor a SINGLE generator (crossing sequence of length 1), all three\nirreps have eigenvalues drawn from `{q, −q⁻¹}`:\n- Symmetric: `lamSym = c·q + d`\n- Standard: eigenvalues of `T(c,d)` = `{c·HA + d ± √Δ}/2`\n  where `Δ = (c·HA+d-d)² + 4c²` — for pp: eigenvalues are `q, −q⁻¹`\n- Alternating: `lamAlt = d − c·q⁻¹`\n\nFor the pp crossing `(c,d) = (1,0)`:\n`lamSym = q`, `lamAlt = −q⁻¹`, and the 2×2 has eigenvalues `q, −q⁻¹`.\nAll channels share the same eigenvalue set.\n\nThis is the formal content of `transfer_matrix_6x6.py` line 233:\n\"the 6×6 does NOT give new information beyond the 2×2 for the\nPER-GENERATOR transfer matrix.\" ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Archimedean.multiAltEigenvalue_append","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Archimedean.multiAltEigenvalue_append","kind":"theorem","line":128,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Archimedean/MultiGeneratorSpectrum.lean#L128-L137","doc":"**Lemma**: the multi-generator alternating eigenvalue is multiplicative. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Archimedean.multiSymEigenvalue_append","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Archimedean.multiSymEigenvalue_append","kind":"theorem","line":117,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Archimedean/MultiGeneratorSpectrum.lean#L117-L126","doc":"**Lemma**: the multi-generator symmetric eigenvalue is multiplicative. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Archimedean.independent_channel_evolution","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Archimedean.independent_channel_evolution","kind":"theorem","line":91,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Archimedean/MultiGeneratorSpectrum.lean#L91-L115","doc":"**Theorem (Independent channel evolution).**\n\nFor a multi-generator product, the 6×6 transfer matrix is\nblock-diagonal:\n```\nT₆(product) = lamSym(product) ⊕ T_std(product) ⊕ T_std(product) ⊕ lamAlt(product)\n```\n\nThe channels evolve independently — the symmetric eigenvalue\ndoes not interact with the standard matrix, etc.\n\nProof: the 6×6 matrix is block-diagonal for each generator\n(by the Wedderburn-Artin decomposition of `H₃(q)`), and the product\nof block-diagonal matrices is block-diagonal. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Archimedean.multiStdMatrix","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Archimedean.multiStdMatrix","kind":"def","line":80,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Archimedean/MultiGeneratorSpectrum.lean#L80-L87","doc":"The **multi-generator standard channel**: the product of 2×2\ntransfer matrices over a crossing sequence.\n\nThis is the same as `matrixChain` from `TMCorrespondence.lean`,\nbut we compute it here in the context of the 6×6 decomposition. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Archimedean.multiAltEigenvalue","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Archimedean.multiAltEigenvalue","kind":"def","line":72,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Archimedean/MultiGeneratorSpectrum.lean#L72-L78","doc":"The **multi-generator alternating eigenvalue**: the product of\nalternating eigenvalues over a crossing sequence.\n\n`lamAlt(product) = ∏_j (d_j − c_j · q⁻¹)` ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Archimedean.multiSymEigenvalue","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Archimedean.multiSymEigenvalue","kind":"def","line":60,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Archimedean/MultiGeneratorSpectrum.lean#L60-L70","doc":"The **multi-generator symmetric eigenvalue**: the product of\nsymmetric eigenvalues over a crossing sequence.\n\nSince the symmetric representation is 1-dimensional, the\ncharacter is a ring homomorphism:\n`χ_sym(σ_{i₁} ⋯ σ_{iₖ}) = q^k`\nMore generally, for crossing `(c_j, d_j)`:\n`lamSym(product) = ∏_j (c_j · q + d_j)` ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Archimedean.altEigenvalue_pp","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Archimedean.altEigenvalue_pp","kind":"theorem","line":56,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Archimedean/MultiGeneratorSpectrum.lean#L56-L58","doc":"For the pp crossing `(c, d) = (1, 0)`: `λ_alt = −q⁻¹`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Archimedean.symEigenvalue_pp","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Archimedean.symEigenvalue_pp","kind":"theorem","line":52,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Archimedean/MultiGeneratorSpectrum.lean#L52-L54","doc":"For the pp crossing `(c, d) = (1, 0)`: `λ_sym = q`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Archimedean.altEigenvalue","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Archimedean.altEigenvalue","kind":"def","line":47,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Archimedean/MultiGeneratorSpectrum.lean#L47-L50","doc":"The **alternating-channel eigenvalue** of a single crossing\n`(c, d)`: `λ_alt = d − c·q⁻¹` (the sign representation evaluates\n`σ ↦ −q⁻¹`). ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Archimedean.symEigenvalue","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Archimedean.symEigenvalue","kind":"def","line":42,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Archimedean/MultiGeneratorSpectrum.lean#L42-L45","doc":"The **symmetric-channel eigenvalue** of a single crossing `(c, d)`:\n`λ_sym = c·q + d` (the 1-dimensional symmetric representation\nevaluates `σ ↦ q`). ","signatureChunk":0}]}